4. EXPIRY OF THE DEVELOPMENT RESTRICTION

The Conversion Date is the first time the Release Authority publicly
makes available an official stable release of the Project designated
1.0.0, including a release tagged v1.0.0.

If the Release Authority skips version 1.0.0, its first official stable
release with a higher version number is the Conversion Date instead.
For this purpose, compare the major, minor, and patch numbers numerically;
an optional leading "v" and build metadata do not affect the comparison.

A qualifying release must be publicly available through an official
Project repository or distribution channel. An official release is treated
as stable unless its version identifier or the Release Authority's release
notice identifies it as an alpha, beta, release candidate, preview, draft,
or other prerelease. Such prereleases do not qualify, even if a filename
or package field contains "1.0.0".

A version change alone, an independently issued fork release, or a release
of a dependency or separately versioned component does not trigger
conversion for the Project. A component release qualifies only if the
Release Authority expressly identifies that component as the Project
covered by this License.

The Release Authority may bring the Conversion Date forward through an
express public notice identifying the Project and the earlier effective
date. Once reached, the Conversion Date cannot be postponed or reversed
by deleting, withdrawing, renaming, or relabeling the release or notice.

There is no separate calendar deadline. Until a qualifying release or
earlier conversion takes effect, the development terms continue to apply.
This License does not require the Release Authority to make a release.

